---
import DocLayout from "../../layouts/DocLayout.astro";

const bodyContent = `<h1>Self-hosting the registry</h1>
  <p class="subtitle">Private networks, roles, identity, policies, audit export and blueprints in the open-source registry, for anyone who runs their own.</p>

  <div class="callout"><strong>Who can use these features.</strong> Every operation in this section needs the registry admin token (some network operations also accept that network&rsquo;s own admin token or an owner/admin role). On the public backbone run by Vulture Labs only the operators hold the admin token, so you can&rsquo;t create or administer networks there. To use these features, run your own rendezvous and point your daemons at it.</div>

  <div class="toc">
    <h4>On this page</h4>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#overview">Overview</a></li>
      <li><a href="#run">Run your own registry</a></li>
      <li><a href="#enable">Enable enterprise mode</a></li>
      <li><a href="#features">Feature summary</a></li>
      <li><a href="#gating">Enterprise gating</a></li>
      <li><a href="#next">What&rsquo;s next</a></li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <h2 id="overview">Overview</h2>

  <p>The rendezvous server (<a href="https://github.com/pilot-protocol/rendezvous">github.com/pilot-protocol/rendezvous</a>, AGPL-3.0) is the registry and beacon that daemons connect to. Besides addresses and trust, it can run <a href="networks">networks</a> with the controls a private deployment needs: role-based access control, identity provider integration, membership policies, structured audit logging, and declarative provisioning through blueprints.</p>

  <p>Plain networks treat membership as a binary boundary - you are either in or out. Networks in enterprise mode add layers on top: <strong>who can do what</strong> (RBAC), <strong>who is allowed in</strong> (identity &amp; directory sync), <strong>what traffic is permitted</strong> (port policies), <strong>what happened</strong> (audit), and <strong>how to set it all up at once</strong> (blueprints).</p>

  <h2 id="run">Run your own registry</h2>

  <p>One <code>rendezvous</code> process serves the registry (TCP) and the beacon (UDP). Set an admin token when you start it; with no token, network creation is disabled.</p>

  <pre><code><span class="comment"># Build and start the registry + beacon</span>
git clone https://github.com/pilot-protocol/rendezvous
<span class="cmd">cd</span> rendezvous &amp;&amp; go build ./cmd/rendezvous
./rendezvous -registry-addr :9000 -beacon-addr :9001 \\
  -store ./registry.json -admin-token "$PILOT_ADMIN_TOKEN"

<span class="comment"># Point a daemon at it</span>
<span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> daemon start --registry registry.example.com:9000 --beacon registry.example.com:9001

<span class="comment"># Admin commands read the registry address and token from the environment</span>
<span class="cmd">export</span> PILOT_REGISTRY=registry.example.com:9000
<span class="cmd">export</span> PILOT_ADMIN_TOKEN=your-admin-token</code></pre>

  <p><code>pilotctl</code> also reads the token from <code>admin_token</code> in <code>~/.pilot/config.json</code>. The registry admin commands sit under <code>pilotctl extras</code>; run it with no arguments for the list. See <a href="/self-hosting/">Self-hosting</a> for running the registry in production.</p>

  <h2 id="enable">Enable enterprise mode</h2>

  <p>Enterprise mode is set per network. Enable it at creation time:</p>

  <pre><code><span class="cmd">pilotctl</span> network create --name prod-fleet --enterprise --join-rule invite</code></pre>

  <p><code>network create</code> needs the registry admin token. <code>--join-rule</code> is <code>open</code> (the default), <code>token</code> (with <code>--token</code>), or <code>invite</code>, which requires <code>--enterprise</code>. The creator node (<code>--node-id</code>) becomes the network&rsquo;s <strong>owner</strong>; enterprise mode is what makes the roles enforceable.</p>

  <h2 id="features">Feature summary</h2>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Feature</th><th>What it does</th><th>Documentation</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><strong>RBAC</strong></td><td>Three-tier roles (owner, admin, member) with distinct permissions. Promote, demote, kick, transfer ownership.</td><td><a href="enterprise-rbac">RBAC &amp; Access Control</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Invites</strong></td><td>Invite agents to invite-only networks; the invited node accepts or rejects. 30-day TTL, inbox cap of 100.</td><td><a href="enterprise-rbac#invites">Invite Flow</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Identity &amp; SSO</strong></td><td>OIDC/JWT validation (RS256/HS256 against a JWKS) and a webhook for your own verification endpoint.</td><td><a href="enterprise-identity">Identity &amp; SSO</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Directory sync</strong></td><td>Map external directory entries and roles to existing agents. Sync does not create agents or add network members.</td><td><a href="enterprise-identity#directory-sync">Directory Sync</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Network policies</strong></td><td>Enforce membership caps, port allowlists, and network descriptions.</td><td><a href="enterprise-policies">Network Policies</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Audit</strong></td><td>Structured audit events (slog JSON), in-memory ring buffer, export to Splunk HEC, CEF/Syslog, or JSON endpoints.</td><td><a href="enterprise-audit">Audit Log &amp; Export</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Webhooks</strong></td><td>Event-driven notifications with retry, dead-letter queue, and Prometheus metrics.</td><td><a href="enterprise-audit#webhooks">Webhooks &amp; DLQ</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Blueprints</strong></td><td>Declarative JSON documents that provision an entire network: name, policies, identity, webhooks, audit export, roles.</td><td><a href="enterprise-blueprints">Blueprints</a></td></tr>
      <tr><td><strong>Key lifecycle</strong></td><td>Rotate agent keys, set expiry dates, and block expired agents from heartbeating.</td><td><a href="enterprise-rbac#authorization-chain">Authorization Chain</a></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2 id="gating">Enterprise gating</h2>

  <p>Some features require enterprise mode on the network. Others work for all networks.</p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>Requires enterprise</th><th>Available to all networks</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td>
          <ul>
            <li>RBAC roles (promote, demote, kick)</li>
            <li>Ownership transfer</li>
            <li>Invite flow</li>
            <li>Directory sync</li>
            <li>Port policies</li>
          </ul>
        </td>
        <td>
          <ul>
            <li>Network create / join / leave / delete</li>
            <li>Membership listing</li>
            <li>Audit log query (global)</li>
            <li>Key rotation</li>
            <li>Hostname &amp; visibility changes</li>
            <li>Tags</li>
            <li>Trust &amp; handshakes</li>
            <li>Per-network admin tokens</li>
            <li>Blueprint provisioning</li>
          </ul>
        </td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <p>Attempting an enterprise operation on a non-enterprise network returns an error. The flag is toggled by the registry&rsquo;s <code>set_network_enterprise</code> RPC (also reachable via the Go SDK&rsquo;s <code>client.Client.SetNetworkEnterprise</code>); membership is preserved across the toggle.</p>

  <p>Note: network creation and deletion, per-network admin tokens and blueprint provisioning are gated by the <strong>admin token</strong>, not by enterprise mode — they work on any network. Blueprint provisioning only <em>enables</em> enterprise when the blueprint explicitly requests it.</p>

  <h2 id="next">What&rsquo;s next</h2>

  <p>Start with the feature most relevant to your deployment:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><a href="enterprise-rbac"><strong>RBAC &amp; Access Control</strong></a> - roles, permissions, invites, and the authorization chain</li>
    <li><a href="enterprise-identity"><strong>Identity &amp; SSO</strong></a> - validate OIDC/JWT natively; bridge other identity systems; sync directories</li>
    <li><a href="enterprise-policies"><strong>Network Policies</strong></a> - membership caps, port allowlists, and metadata</li>
    <li><a href="enterprise-audit"><strong>Audit Log &amp; Export</strong></a> - structured logging, export to SIEMs, webhooks</li>
    <li><a href="enterprise-blueprints"><strong>Blueprints</strong></a> - provision entire networks from a single JSON document</li>
  </ul>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>See also:</strong> <a href="networks">Networks</a> - the foundation these features build on. <a href="cli-reference">CLI Reference</a> - full command listing, including the registry admin commands.
  </div>`;
